import json
DEV = [
"D7. Termination coding unit and codebook v0.3 (17 October 2026). The coding item became the polity rather than the predecessor-successor pair, because the outcome is how each polity ended. Codebook v0.3 is v0.2 with the coder naming the successor (category S2 and code NA removed) and an end-year estimate added. The pilot items were recoded fresh under v0.3 and kept in the sample; a sensitivity analysis excludes the 35 in-frame polities that appeared in the pilot.",
"D8. Coding scope and double coding. Only the 429 polities that enter at least one model were coded (those with a mappable narrative tradition, or a known Seshat value for moralizing enforcement of rulers or for moralizing supernatural punishment), not all 864. Each was coded once; a random 20 percent (86 items, seed 20261017) was coded again by independent instances, and the 7 disagreements on termination type were adjudicated by a third instance. Section 5 requires two coders on every item.",
"D9. Covariates. Most Seshat values are not dated within a polity, so covariates are polity-level averages rather than century-level time-varying values. Agricultural productivity and kinship intensity are not in the public Seshat API and are omitted from H2. Social complexity is the first principal component of nine characteristics built from current Seshat variables (67.5 percent of variance on average), and military technology is the summed within-category share of 45 warfare variables in six categories; both simplify Turchin et al. (2018, 2022). Missing covariates were imputed 50 times by chained equations (Bayesian ridge with posterior draws), with the event indicator, the Nelson-Aalen cumulative hazard and macro-region in the imputation model.",
"D10. Models. Shared frailty by NGA and the spatial covariance term could not be fitted with the software available (no R). They are replaced by standard errors clustered on narrative tradition, a tradition-level permutation test for the CCI as the primary inference, and sensitivity analyses with macro-region strata and region clustering. Fine-Gray models were fitted with custom code checked against statsmodels and by simulation. Two model fits did not converge (one imputation in each of two H4a models) and were dropped from pooling. After the analysis script was frozen (SHA-256 beginning a2fcd350), the only changes were for numerical robustness; the diff is archived with the code.",
"D11. Hypotheses not tested in this run: H3 (the dictionary CCI is constant within a tradition, so it cannot be lagged within polities); H4b (the conquest-episode coding was not done); H6 (Study 3, see D5); H9a (it needs new coding of ethnographies, and eHRAF is not accessible here); the narrative Ruler Constraint item of H8 (a language model cannot code it blind); and Study 2 (the Berezkin motif data were not obtained in this run).",
"D12. H9b. The D statistic was computed on a global tree assembled from the D-PLACE Glottolog classification trees (topology only, unit branch lengths, families joined at a root, polytomies resolved at random), with traits collapsed to one value per language. No cooperative-norm codes exist for the same societies, so the comparison in H9b's falsification condition cannot be made; only the signal of deity belief is estimated.",
]
H13 = "13. Results of the exploratory run (17 October 2026)"
S = [
("p", "Everything in this section is exploratory under Deviations D1 to D12. No test survived the Holm-Bonferroni correction, and no result here is confirmatory. Code, data extracts and logs are kept with the analysis files (polities_frame.csv, final_codes.csv, cci_traditions.csv, covariates.csv, analysis.py, results.json, h9b_results.json)."),
("h", "13.1 Coding frame and termination codes"),
("p", "Of 864 polities in the current Seshat release, 430 enter at least one model; 429 were coded and 1 is right-censored at the present. Termination types across the 429: continuity 164, conquest 163, internal collapse 67 (fragmentation or civil war 30, abandonment 2, revolution or coup 35), absorption 11, population replacement 2, secession 2, voluntary union 2, insufficient evidence 18. The sample therefore holds 245 terminations of known type, close to the 254 the power analysis required; but only 176 of them fall in polities with a scorable narrative tradition."),
("p", "Agreement on the 86 double-coded items: termination type 79 of 86 (Krippendorff alpha 0.89 on full codes, 0.92 on collapsed categories, 0.90 on termination yes or no); succession category alpha 0.91; end of central authority 0.96; prior internal crisis 0.80; confidence 0.47. Both coders are instances of one model, so these figures measure its consistency, not human reliability (D3). An audit of 20 random items found that the cited pages fully supported 16 codes and partly supported 4, with none contradicted; 5 had date discrepancies of 1 to 30 years between code and source."),
("h", "13.2 Cooperative Content Index"),
("p", "CCI by tradition (mean z-score across the seven MAC-D virtue domains), from lowest to highest: Daoism -0.39, Buddhism -0.38, Judaism -0.23, Christianity -0.21, Zoroastrianism -0.20, Egyptian -0.18, Hinduism -0.07, Confucianism -0.05, Roman 0.07, Greek 0.09, Norse-Germanic 0.09, Mesopotamian 0.09, Jainism 0.25, Islam 0.41, Shinto 0.69. Corpora range from 21,708 tokens (Mesopotamian) to 790,046 (Christianity). The ranking shows the measure's main weakness: Shinto scores highest mainly because the Kojiki's genealogies are dense with kinship words (113 Family-domain terms per 10,000 tokens, about twice any other tradition), which is salience, not endorsement of family obligations (D4). Of the 430 framed polities, 261 have a tradition under the primary rule; 253 enter the H1 sample, falling into 19 distinct tradition combinations from 15 traditions."),
("h", "13.3 Study 1: narrative content and endurance"),
("p", "H1 (content predicts endurance): hazard ratio per SD of CCI 1.08 (95 percent CI 0.90 to 1.28; tradition-level permutation p = 0.51; 253 polities, 176 terminations). Not supported. Equivalence is not established either, because the 90 percent CI (0.93 to 1.25) extends past the 1.11 bound; the data exclude a protective effect stronger than about 0.90 per SD but not a small effect in either direction."),
("p", "H2 (robust to drivers of power): adjusted hazard ratio 1.08 (0.92 to 1.25; permutation p = 0.47). Not supported. Of the controls, only iron weapons reached p < 0.05 (hazard ratio 2.77, 1.03 to 7.42)."),
("p", "H4a (internal versus external exit): Fine-Gray sub-hazard ratio for internal collapse 1.15 (0.80 to 1.66; permutation p = 0.28; 49 events) and for conquest 1.04 (0.78 to 1.38; 117 events); cause-specific estimates 1.18 and 1.05. Not supported: CCI does not lower the internal-collapse hazard."),
("p", "H5 (vessel independence): with moralizing supernatural punishment in the model the CCI hazard ratio is 1.07 (0.93 to 1.24), unchanged, but there is no CCI effect for the vessel to absorb. The moralizing punishment coefficient itself is uninformative (hazard ratio 8.0, 0.8 to 80), because 85 percent of coded polities have it."),
("p", "H7 (temporal invariance): era-specific hazard ratios (adjusted) are 0.58 before 500 BCE (34 polities, 18 terminations), 1.03 from 500 BCE to 1500 CE and 1.09 after 1500 CE; interaction Wald p = 0.085 adjusted (0.007 unadjusted; Holm-adjusted 0.43). The early-era estimate rests almost entirely on two traditions (Mesopotamian 13 polities, Egyptian 12), and the Wald test ignores the covariance between the two interaction terms, so this is not evidence for or against temporal invariance."),
("p", "Robustness. The proportional-hazards assumption holds (Schoenfeld rho 0.05, p = 0.55). Dropping one tradition at a time gives hazard ratios from 0.96 (without Islam) to 1.14 (without Buddhism). Other sensitivity analyses give 0.95 to 1.16: complete cases 1.05 (n = 96), the virtue-minus-vice index 0.99, coder end dates 1.06, macro-region strata 1.07, excluding pilot polities 1.04, and assigning traditions by official religion 1.16 (1.01 to 1.33). Across the 72-specification curve the median hazard ratio is 1.07; 15 percent of specifications fall below 1, none significantly, while 7 of 72 (all using official-religion assignment) are significant in the direction opposite to the prediction. A separate script written by an independent instance, using statsmodels, reproduced the H1 sample (253 polities, 176 terminations, 49 internal collapses, 117 conquests) and the H1 hazard ratio (1.0755) exactly."),
("h", "13.4 H8: rulers under moral sanction"),
("p", "Among 363 polities with a known Seshat code for moralizing enforcement of rulers (294 present, 69 absent; 60 internal collapses), the cause-specific hazard ratio for internal collapse is 3.04 (1.18 to 7.85) and the Fine-Gray sub-hazard ratio 2.20 (0.82 to 5.93); after adjustment for the H2 covariates it is 1.32 (0.34 to 5.12). The conquest sub-hazard is also higher (1.89, 1.11 to 3.24). H8a is not supported: the unadjusted association runs opposite to the prediction and vanishes with adjustment, which suggests that the code tracks scale and era rather than ruler conduct. H8b: mean crisis severity at a polity-ending crisis is 0.81 where rulers faced moralizing enforcement (n = 43) and 1.00 where they did not (n = 6; Mann-Whitney p = 0.50), too few cases to test. The Seshat variable measures supernatural sanction on rulers, not service-based leadership, so H8's core claim remains untested."),
("h", "13.5 H9b: belief in a moralizing high god"),
("p", "Across 692 languages (775 Ethnographic Atlas societies), a moralizing high god (EA034 = 4) is present in 23.6 percent. Its phylogenetic signal is strong: D = -0.20 (p < 0.001 against a random distribution; p = 0.92 against a Brownian threshold model), meaning it is at least as clustered by descent as a trait evolving by Brownian motion; across 10 random resolutions of the tree's polytomies D ranged from -0.23 to -0.16. A high god of any kind (64.0 percent) is less clustered (D = 0.19; range 0.16 to 0.23) but still far from random. The odds of a moralizing high god rise 2.37-fold per level of jurisdictional hierarchy above the local community (95 percent CI 1.86 to 3.02), from 6.3 percent in societies with no such level to 61.1 percent in those with four. Belief in a moralizing God therefore travels with descent and with political scale, as expected of a trait spread by inheritance and contact rather than discovered independently. Because H9a was not run, the convergence of cooperative moral content itself was not tested here."),
("h", "13.6 Interpretation"),
("p", "In this run, the moral content of canonical texts, as scored by a fixed dictionary, does not predict how long polities lasted, how they ended, or whether they collapsed from within. The estimates rule out a moderate or large protective effect but cannot exclude a small one. This is a weak test of the narrative selection hypothesis, for five reasons: the effective sample is 15 traditions, not 253 polities; the dictionary measures how often moral domains are mentioned, not whether a text endorses them; polities are assigned the canon of their dominant religion, which ignores local and oral narratives; outcomes were coded by one model without human validation; and two thirds of terminations are conquests, which the hypothesis does not claim content prevents. The result most robust to these limits is H9b: belief in a moralizing God tracks descent and the scale of political organization. A confirmatory test needs human coders validating at least a subset of termination codes, an endorsement-based content measure coded blind on redacted passages, far more traditions including oral ones (Study 2), and Stage 1 review before any of it is run."),
]
json.dump(dict(DEV=DEV, H13=H13, S=S), open('/root/analysis/out/results_text.json', 'w'))
alltext = ' '.join(DEV) + H13 + ' '.join(t for _, t in S)
assert '—' not in alltext and '–' not in alltext
print(len(alltext))
